i-budget makes it more likely  that you'll follow through  because so many budgets demand that we  uh  excessively line itemize everything to  the point where we know exactly how much  money we're spending on toothpaste and  dog food  and like who has time for that and who  can stick with that  you know it's kind of like uh um  those those apps where i've downloaded  so many apps where i'm supposed to input  every single thing that i eat  all day and i stick with it for maybe a  week and a half and then i just i can't  yeah get at lunch yeah it can get  overwhelming you're right because  um yeah it's it's tracking every single  penny  um but it sounds like and correct me if  i'm wrong you prioritize spending even  even over paying the bills um  so it's the philosophy huh you just  you're not spending over  over paying the bills but you work  everything else in there and you've also  expanded your definition  of what savings is like you just said  before so  that makes sense of course and so  speaking of saving  what are some very practical ways people  can start saving right now women can  start  saving so this is a great question so  first of all  uh when i think of savings as a  framework um  there are fundamentally there are two  choices  you you what you want to do is grow the  gap  between what you earn and what you spend  and oftentimes the word savings has this  connotation of a reduction in spending  but i want to move away from that  connotation and focus on  increasing the gap between income and  spending and once we look at it  in that context then the best way to for  some people  depending on who you are um the the best  way to  save maybe to earn more um that's not  true for everybody and that's not true  in all contexts but  i want to make that clear because the  word saving just  it does have that connotation of a  reduction of spending and that's not  necessarily  always the best approach for everyone so  uh  if the goal is to increase the gap  between what you earn and what you spend  there there are fundamentally three ways  to do it you can either earn more  you could spend less or you could do a  combination of the two  now if we do want to talk about the  spend less side of the equation we'll  discuss that first  um oftentimes the discussion  in tends to center around consumer  discretionary purchases  so i think we tend to give undue focus  to these small tweaks that we can make  oh don't  go get manicures uh you know  and unfortunately those are  you know those are sort of the expenses  that happen in the margins  um and they can they're  they because of salience like it's easy  for them to come to mind and they're  easy adjustments to make and so  oftentimes the discussion i think  over focuses on those when in fact  the majority of spending in most u.s  households  are the big three housing transportation  food  so if you really want to cut back on  spending  those three areas you know reducing the  size of your home  um radically altering your  transportation maybe you go from a  two-car family to a one-hour family  you know harder adjustments to make i  mean we're talking about  selling your home and moving to  somewhere um to something that's that's  a thousand square feet smaller like  that's not an easy shift  right it's much more meaningful than  um you know than some of the consumer  discretionary stuff that tends to get  i think overemphasized okay uh you know  what those are all  pretty practical and they may be things  that people don't think about all the  time  um but we have a quick question from the  audience someone wants to know how  to invest your stemi check  all right so how to so fundamentally how  to invest your stimulus check is  how to invest how to invest any amount  of money particularly  you know if if you weren't expecting  this money and you don't need it to pay  your bills  right and so in order to approach that  the first question  that i've got for for everyone who's  wo